Congenital heart defects
Disabilities & Congenital Disorders
Central cyanosis, Pathological heart murmur, Poor feeding/Failure to thrive, Exertional dyspnoea

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DCD-014
Reason for Visit: Acute hypercyanotic spell ("Tet spell") and severe respiratory distress.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Infant and parents
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a 6-month-old infant with a known ventricular septal defect and right ventricular outflow tract obstruction who developed sudden, deep central cyanosis, profound tachypnoea, and inconsolable crying following a bowel movement. The assessment could not be delayed until the next in-hours period due to the life-threatening nature of an unresolving hypercyanotic spell, requiring immediate positioning, oxygenation, and medical stabilization to prevent hypoxic brain injury or cardiac arrest.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: He strained to poop, suddenly turned dark blue around the lips, and is gasping for air.
History of Present Illness: The parents report a 30-minute history of acute, severe respiratory distress and deep bluish discolouration of the lips and oral mucosa (central cyanosis). The episode was triggered by the physical exertion of a bowel movement. The infant has a known congenital heart defect characterized by a pathological heart murmur and a history of poor feeding and failure to thrive. During the current episode, the parents note profound exertional dyspnoea, extreme agitation, and profuse diaphoresis. Previous minor spells resolved spontaneously, but this episode is prolonged and worsening.
Functional Status: Dependent infant; growth restricted.
Social/Safety Concerns: Immediate risk of catastrophic hypoxic brain injury or death.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 80/45 mmHg | HR: 185 bpm | Temp: 36.9 C | RR: 55 bpm | SpO2: 68% | Wt: 5.5 Kg
General: Infant is profoundly cyanotic (lips, mucosa, nail beds), highly agitated, and exhibiting severe respiratory distress with marked sternal retractions.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular exam reveals severe tachycardia. A previously loud systolic murmur is now notably diminished, indicating severe right ventricular outflow tract spasm and right-to-left shunting. Femoral pulses are palpable but weak. Respiratory exam shows clear lung fields with profound tachypnoea. Neurological exam shows an agitated infant with normal pupillary reflexes but decreasing level of interaction. Clubbing of the fingers is noted, consistent with chronic hypoxaemia.
Environmental Observations: Parents were holding the infant flat on his back, worsening the venous return dynamics.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular cardiac medications prescribed yet; awaiting surgical repair.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Congenital heart defects (Tetralogy of Fallot - presenting with acute prolonged hypercyanotic "Tet spell") - Status: Acute, critical complication
Differentials:

Acute Bronchiolitis/Pneumonia - Ruled out/unlikely due to the clear lung fields, absence of fever, sudden onset associated with straining, and the specific clinical phenotype of profound central cyanosis out of proportion to the respiratory work.

Seizure Disorder - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of rhythmic clonic movements or post-ictal state; the agitation is clearly driven by profound acute hypoxia.

Choking/Foreign Body Aspiration - Ruled out/unlikely as the airway is patent, there is no stridor, and the onset was tied to exertion (Valsalva) rather than feeding/playing with small objects.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Extreme (Life-threatening emergency)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The infant presented with a critical, unresolving hypercyanotic "Tet spell," characterized by profound central cyanosis (SpO2 68%) and severe distress following a Valsalva maneuver. The objective loss of the characteristic murmur confirmed near-total right ventricular outflow tract obstruction causing massive right-to-left shunting of deoxygenated blood. This assessment could not be delayed until the next in-hours period, nor could the patient wait for an ambulance without immediate intervention, because untreated profound hypoxia rapidly leads to irreversible neurological damage, syncope, and cardiac arrest. Immediate in-home mechanical and pharmacological maneuvers were mandatory to break the spasm.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Immediately positioned the infant in a strict knee-to-chest posture to increase systemic vascular resistance. Administered high-flow supplemental oxygen via non-rebreather mask. Administered a micro-dose of intranasal fentanyl to provide rapid anxiolysis and reduce sympathetic outflow, successfully breaking the spasm and improving SpO2 to 88% and restoring the loud systolic murmur. Activated 000 for emergency transport.
Positive Behavioral Change: When you immediately push his knees hard into his chest during a crying fit, you force blood into his lungs; therefore, making this your first automatic reflex saves his life. When you keep him calm and avoid letting him cry for prolonged periods, you prevent the heart spasm; prioritizing soothing over routine tasks minimizes his daily risk.
Home-Based Services (Optional): N/A - requires immediate hospital admission.
DME/Safety (Optional): N/A.
Follow-up: Handover provided to the emergency department team.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Patient is currently being transported to the Emergency Department via ambulance under my direct supervision.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The underlying structural heart defect remains; medical stabilization is temporary pending definitive surgical correction.
Clinical Course: The spell was broken with medication and positioning, but the infant remains at critical risk for recurrent, potentially fatal spells until surgical intervention occurs.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

During future episodes, place the infant in the knee-to-chest position immediately and call 000. Do not wait to see if it resolves.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The exertion caused a muscle spasm in his heart, blocking blood from reaching the lungs and turning him blue. Pushing his knees up squeezed his blood vessels, forcing blood back into the lungs, while the nasal spray relaxed the heart muscle.
Actionable Steps: Learn the strict knee-to-chest maneuver. Anticipate and prevent prolonged crying. Follow all instructions from the pediatric cardiology team.

